<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Amazing Community – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Amazing Community</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> This School Has Been Here a Long Time!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Edit short texts with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write short emails describing community events and activities using sentence starters (e.g., 'Today, I went to...' 'My favorite part was...'). Can draft letters describing upcoming events (e.g., 'The school has organized a community clean-up for this weekend.'). Can edit and revise with peers short descriptions of community events (e.g., 'Add more details about the market.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying a picture of an old school building or a community event. Students are asked to brainstorm and write down 2-3 words or short phrases about what they see or imagine happened there a long time ago. (e.g., 'old school', 'children play', 'festival last month').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a short, simple sentence about the picture on the board, for example: "This school has been here for many years." Then, the teacher models how to add a detail or correct a common error (e.g., "This beautiful school has been here for many years."). The teacher also models using sentence starters like "Today, I went to..." or "My favorite part was..." related to a recent community event.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is our task for today? Are we going to speak or write?" (Expected answer: "Write.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we edit a text, do we find mistakes or ignore them?" (Expected answer: "Find mistakes.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple email written to a friend, describing a recent community festival or a planned school event (e.g., a community clean-up). The email uses present simple, past simple (with 'yesterday,' 'last month'), and present perfect ('I have worked here for five years'). It includes vocabulary such as 'community,' 'festival,' 'park,' 'market,' 'vendor,' 'delicious,' 'share,' 'enjoy,' 'volunteer.' Compound nouns like 'neighborhood' and 'police officer' are present.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students can interpret written texts to identify main ideas and specific details about community events and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the email individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main topic of the email?" (Teacher guides to: "A community festival" or "A school event").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students decide if statements about the email are true or false (e.g., "The festival was yesterday." "The school has organized a new market for this weekend.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match roles (e.g., 'vendor,' 'musician') with activities described in the email.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing writing sentences and short descriptions using present simple, past simple, and present perfect tenses. Using vocabulary related to community roles, events, and descriptions (e.g., 'community,' 'festival,' 'school,' 'teacher,' 'carpenter,' 'cook,' 'share,' 'delicious,' 'friendly,' 'every day,' 'last month,' 'have worked').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences using target vocabulary and grammar. (e.g., "Last year, the ______ (community/school) _______ (celebrate/celebrated) the festival with _______ (delicious/sweet) food." "The artist _____ (has worked/work) in our _____ (neighborhood/park) for many _____ (year/years)." They also practice forming questions in present simple: "What _____ (do/does) the _____ (gardener/teacher) do at the _____ (school/park)?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Description:</b> In pairs, students receive a prompt (e.g., "Describe a typical day at your school a long time ago" or "Write about a recent community event"). They must use at least two time expressions (e.g., 'every day,' 'yesterday') and 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., 'school,' 'teacher,' 'share,' 'enjoy'). The teacher provides sentence starters like "Every day, students..." or "Last week, our community...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Check & Pre-editing:</b> Students exchange their guided descriptions from activity 2 with a different partner. They read each other's texts and circle any words they don't understand or sentences that seem incomplete. This is a preliminary step to prepare them for the editing task, focusing on clarity and basic understanding.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Amazing Community".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Email Writing and Editing.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work individually to draft a short email (5-7 sentences) to a friend, describing a community event they recently visited or an upcoming event at their school. They must include at least one detail about a community role ('teacher,' 'vendor,' 'police officer'), use at least one past simple and one present simple sentence, and employ 2-3 time expressions ('yesterday,' 'last month,' 'every day,' 'this weekend'). They can use sentence starters like 'Today, I went to...', 'My favorite part was...', or 'The school has organized a community clean-up for this weekend.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once drafted, students pair up with a new partner. They exchange their emails. Each student then acts as an editor for their partner's email. Using a simple checklist (e.g., "Did my partner use past simple correctly?", "Are there any missing words?", "Is the email easy to understand?", "Are there enough details?"), they identify areas for improvement or errors. They write suggestions on the email or on a separate note (e.g., 'Add more details about the market.', 'Check the verb tense for "cook".').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, students discuss their feedback with their partners and revise their own emails based on the suggestions received.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly relates to sharing information about "Our Amazing Community" and events that "This School Has Been Here a Long Time!" has hosted or will host.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during peer editing and collection of revised emails for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their final, revised email describing a community event or school activity. The teacher will also briefly observe peer-editing interactions to check for effective feedback exchange.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Cohesion:</b> Is the email easy to understand? Is the message clear?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., 'community,' 'festival,' 'delicious,' 'volunteer') used appropriately?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Appropriate use of present simple, past simple, and present perfect (A2.1 level expectations).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Does the email describe a community event/school activity and include details as requested?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Editing Effectiveness (Observation):</b> Did students provide constructive feedback and make revisions based on peer suggestions?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you learned about writing a good email today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you helped your partner improve in their writing?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did working with a partner help you make your writing better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What grammar point (present simple, past simple, present perfect) do you need to practice more in your writing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The peer editing activity was engaging and effective in achieving the objective. Students showed good understanding of present simple and past simple, but some struggled with the present perfect tense and question formation. Scaffolds like sentence frames and checklists helped guide their writing and editing. For next time, I need to dedicate more explicit instruction and targeted practice to the present perfect, possibly with more visual aids or examples related to school history (e.g., 'The school has celebrated this festival for 50 years.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing and editing practice will directly support the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), where students will need to consolidate information and summarize a community event or historical fact about the school for a 21st Century Skills Project, requiring clear, accurate written communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>